.Dd May 9, 2026
.Dt XMENU 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xmenu
.Nd desktop menu for X11
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm xmenu
.Op Fl leader Ar WINDOWID
.Op Fl monitor Ar MONITOR
.Op Fl name Ar NAME
.Op Fl position Ns | Ns Fl geometry Ar GEOMETRY
.Op Fl rootgrab Oo Ar MODIFIER Ns Cm - Oc Ns Ar BUTTON
.Op Fl title Ar TITLE
.Op Fl windowed
.Op Fl xrm Ar RESOURCESTRING
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a desktop menu for X11.
It reads a list of newline-separated entries from standard input;
pops up a menu listing those entries for the user to select one;
and writes the selected entry into standard output.
An entry gets selected by releasing the mouse pointer over it.
.Pp
Entries can be organized hierarchically into nested menus,
so that selecting a non-terminal entry pops up a sub-menu with further entries.
.Pp
Entries can be disabled,
which causes them to be displayed with dimmer colors.
Disabled entries cannot be selected.
.Sh SYNTAX
Each entry read from standard input has the following tab-separated format
(bracket groups optional elements):
.D1 Oo Cm \et Ns ... Oc Oo Ar IMAGE Cm \et Ns ... Oc Ar LABEL Oo Cm \et Ns ... Ar OUTPUT Oc Cm "\en"
or
.D1 Oo Cm \et Ns ... Oc Oo Ar IMAGE Cm \et Ns ... Oc Cm "\(aq\(aq" Oo Cm \et Ns ... Ar OUTPUT Oc Cm "\en"
or
.D1 Oo Cm \et Ns ... Oc Oo Cm ":\)" Oc Cm "\en"
.Pp
The components are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "XXXXXX"
.It Cm \et
The initial tabs indicate the menu hierarchy:
entries indented with a tab are placed in a submenu of the preceding entry not indented.
An entry without initial tabs is a top-level entry.
Tabs after elements are used to separate them.
.It Ar IMAGE
Sing of the form
.Qq Ic "IMG:/path/to/image.png" .
It specifies the path to an image file to be shown as icon at the left of the entry.
If the path does not begin with
.Qq Pa / ,
.Qq Pa ./ ,
or
.Qq Pa ../ ,
the file is searched on the paths specified in the
.Ev ICONPATH
environment variable.
.It Ar LABEL
Sing that will be shown as an entry in the menu.
An entry without label or with a single colon
.Pq Ic \&:
as label is considered a separator and is drawn as a thin line in the menu
separating the entry above from the entry below.
An entry whose label is equal to two apostrophes
.Qq Ic "''"
does not appear on the menu, it's an alternative version for the previous entry
when the user clicks with the middle mouse button.
.It Ar OUTPUT
String that will be output after selecting the entry.
If an entry does not have tabs after the label, its label is used as its output.
.Pp
If the output is empty or is a colon
.Pq Ic \&: ,
the entry cannot be selected.
.Pp
If the output begins with a slash,
it is interpreted as the path to a directory.
Entering this entry pops up a menu containing the entries of that directory,
so the user can navigate the contents of the directory with
.Nm .
If an file is selected, its absolute path is output prefixed with
.Qq Ic "file:" .
A directory's path can be output by selecting it with the middle mouse button.
.It Cm "\en"
The newline terminates the entry specification.
.El
.Sh USAGE
.Bl -tag -width "Left-/right-click" -compact
.It Ic Left- Ns / Ns Ic right-click
.Bl -bullet -compact -width ""
.It 
On dashed topmost entry,
tear the menu off.
.It
On entry with right-pointing triangle,
pop up submenu.
.It
On regular entry ,
print output for entry.
.It
Outside a menu,
closes any popped up menu.
.El
.It Ic Middle-click
Print alternate output for entry (if any).
.It Ic End
Selects the last entry in the menu.
.It Ic Esc
Go to the menu above in the hierarchy or exit
.It Ic Home
Selects the first entry in the menu.
.It Ic Left
Go to the menu above in the hierarchy.
.It Ic Right , Enter
Select the highlighted entry.
.It Ic Shift-Tab , Down
Cycle through the entries in the reverse direction.
.It Ic Tab , Up
Cycle through the entries in the regular direction.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl leader Ar WINDOW
Make the menu windows transient for the given
.Ar WINDOW .
That is, notify the window manager that the menus are bound to the given leader
.Ar WINDOW .
This also makes
.Nm
close when the
.Ar WINDOW
is destroyed.
.It Fl monitor Ar MONITOR
Menu placement
.Po
as specified by the
.Fl position
argument
.Pc
is relative to the given monitor,
rather than relative to the entire X screen.
For a list of available monitors, run
.Ql xrandr --listmonitors .
.It Fl name Ar NAME
Specifiy the application name under which resources are to be obtained,
rather than the execultable file name.
.Ar NAME
should not contain
.Dq ".\&"
or
.Dq "*\&"
characters.
.It Fl position Xo
.Cm "±" Ns Ar "X"
.Cm "±" Ns Ar "Y"
.Xc
Set the position to open
.Nm
at.
Without this option,
.Nm
pops up next to the cursor.
The
.Ar X
and
.Ar Y
arguments, and the plus or minus sign preceding them, are obligatory.
The percent signs, and the part from the at-sign to the end are optional.
.Bl -bullet -compact -width ""
.It Fl position Ar GEOMETRY , Fl geometry Ar GEOMETRY
Set the position to open
.Nm
at, and (optionally) its size.
Refer to the
.Xr X 7
manual, at the section
.Sx GEOMETRY SPECIFICATIONS
for more information on the syntax for this option.
.It Fl rootgrab Oo Ar MODIFIER Ns Cm - Oc Ns Ar BUTTON
This option requires an argument of the form
.Oo Ar MODIFIER Ns Cm - Oc Ns Ar BUTTON ;
where
.Ar MODIFIER
is
.Cm Mod1
to
.Cm Mod5 ,
or
.Cm Alt
(equivalent to
.Cm Mod1 ) ,
or
.Cm Super
(equivalent to
.Cm Mod4 ) ;
and
.Ar BUTTON
is the number of a mouse button.
When this option is used,
.Nm
listens to button presses on the root window,
and shows the menu when the given button is pressed on the root window
or when that button is pressed together with the given modifier on any window.
This option makes
.Nm
run continuously;
so it should be used when
.Nm
is invoked in background on a X11 startup file
(like
.Pa "~/.xinitrc"
or
.Pa "~/.xsession" ) .
This option cannot be used together with
.Fl windowed.
.It Fl title Ar TITLE
Set the title of the menu window, when torn off.
.It Fl windowed
Asks the window manager to draw a border around the menu.
This makes
.Nm
start torn off.
.It Fl xrm Ar RESOURCESTRING
Set resource name and value pairs to override any defaults,
and any resource read from the X server.
.El
.Sh RESOURCES
All resources apply to the toplevel
.Qq Ic XMenu
class or the name specified by the
.Fl name
command line option
.Po
.Qq Ic xmenu
by default
.Pc .
Refer to the
.Xr X 7
manual, at the section
.Sx RESOURCES
for more information on X resources.
.Bl -column XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX XXX
.It Sy Resource name                Ta Sy Default value  Ta Sy Type
.It Dv activeBackground  Ta Dv #3465A4   Ta color
.It Dv activeForeground  Ta Dv #FFFFFF   Ta color
.It Dv alignment         Ta Dv left      Ta Brq Qo Dv left Qc , Qo Dv center Qc , Qo Dv right Qc
.It Dv background        Ta Dv #313131   Ta color
.It Dv borderWidth       Ta Dv 1         Ta size (in pixels)
.It Dv font Ns / Ns Dv faceName     Ta (system's default)  Ta xft-font Sy OR No xldf-font
.It Dv fontSize Ns / Ns Dv faceSize Ta (system's default)  Ta size (in points)
.It Dv foreground        Ta Dv #FFFFFF   Ta color
.It Dv gap               Ta Dv 0         Ta distance (in pixels)
.It Dv maxItems          Ta (computed)   Ta positive integer
.It Dv opacity           Ta 1.0          Ta Bq 0.0 , 1.0
.It Dv shadowThickness   Ta Dv 1         Ta size (in pixels)
.It Dv tearOff           Ta Dv false     Ta boolean
.It Dv topShadowColor    Ta (computed)   Ta boolean
.It Dv bottomShadowColor Ta (computed)   Ta boolean
.El
.Pp
For the syntax of color resources,
refer to the
.Xr X 7
manual, at the section
.Sx COLOR NAMES .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic alignment
If set to
.Qq Ic left ,
.Qq Ic center ,
or
.Qq Ic right ,
text is aligned to the left, center, or right of the menu, respectively.
By default, text is aligned to the left.
.It Ic background , activeBackground
The color of the background for the menu and its selected entry,
respectively.
.It Ic borderColor
The color of the border around the menu.
.It Ic borderWidth
The size in pixels of the border around the menu.
.It Ic font Pq synonym: Ic faceName
The font for drawing text.
.Pp
If it is prefixed with
.Qq Ic xft: ,
it is a
.Em FontConfig
font pattern;
and the Xft library is used to draw text.
Fallback fonts, for missing glyphs, can be specified by delimiting the fonts with commas.
Refer to the
.Xr fonts.conf 5
manual, at the section
.Sx FONT NAMES ,
for the syntax of font pattern strings.
.Pp
If it is prefixed with
.Qq Ic x:
or
.Qq Ic x11: ,
it is a
.Em X Logical Font Description
.Pq Em XLFD
font pattern;
and the X11 library is used to draw text.
Refer to the
.Xr fonts.conf 5
manual, at the section
.Sx FONT NAMES ,
for the syntax of font pattern strings.
.Pp
If no prefix is given,
.Nm
first tries the resource as a XLFD pattern, and then as a Xft pattern.
.It Ic fontSize Pq synonym: Ic faceSize
The size, in points of the font.
This only affects
.Xr Xft 3
fonts.
.It Ic foreground , activeForeground
The color of the label text on the menu and its selected entry,
respectively.
If not set, these resources are a contrasting color computed from
.Ic background
and
.Ic activeBackground ,
respectively.
.It Ic gap
The gap, in pixels, between the menus.
.It Ic maxItems
Maximum number of entries to be displayed in a menu.
If a menu has more than this number of entries, they will be scrolled with arrow buttons.
The actual number of entries displayed may be lower to fit the screen.
.It Ic opacity
Background opacity as a floating point number between
0.0 (transparent) and 1.0 (opaque) inclusive.
.It Ic shadowThickness
The size in pixels of the Motif-like 3D relief.
.It Ic tearOff
If
.Qq Ic true ,
creates a dashed line on the top of each non-windowed menu to tear them off into a new windowed menu.
.It Ic topShadowColor , bottomShadowColor
The color of the top/light and bottom/dark parts of the Motif-like 3D relief.
If not set, these resources are computed from
.Ic background .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The following environment variables affect the execution of
.Nm .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev DISPLAY
The display to start
.Nm
on.
.It Ev ICONPATH
A colon-separated list of directories used to search for the location of image files.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following script illustrates the use of
.Nm .
The output is redirected to
.Xr sh 1 ,
creating a command to be run by the shell.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$!/bin/sh

xmenu -f <<EOF | sh &
Applications
	IMG:./web.png	Web Browser	firefox
	IMG:./gimp.png	Image Editor	gimp
Terminal (xterm)			xterm
Terminal (urxvt)			urxvt
Terminal (st)				st

Navigate Home				/home/user

Shutdown				poweroff
Reboot					reboot
EOF
.Ed
.Pp
For example, by selecting "Applications", a new menu will appear.
Selecting "Web Browser" in the new menu opens firefox.
.Pp
The
.Fl f
option makes it possible to browse the contents of the home directory by selecting the "Navigate Home" entry.
This will open a submenu listing all the entries of the home directory.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xclickroot 1 ,
.Xr X 7
